Suppose we have a view defined as:
CREATE VIEW instructor_info AS
SELECT ID, name, building
FROM instructor, department
WHERE instructor.dept_name = department.dept_name;
If we execute:
INSERT INTO instructor_info VALUES ('69987', 'White', 'Taylor');
What will be the values of the attributes in the base relations (instructor and department) that are not included in the view?

CREATE VIEW instructor_info AS
SELECT ID, name, building
FROM instructor, department
WHERE instructor.dept_name = department.dept_name;

INSERT INTO instructor_info VALUES ('69987', 'White', 'Taylor');

VIEW dept_view;

SELECT * FROM dept_view;

SHOW dept_view;

DISPLAY dept_view;

explanation:
Attributes not specified in the INSERT will take NULL values if no constraints exist; otherwise, the operation fails.
9. A view named dept_view is created as:
CREATE VIEW dept_view AS
SELECT dept_name, budget
FROM department;
Which query will display the contents of this view?
a)


VIEW dept_view;


b)


SELECT * FROM dept_view;


c)


SHOW dept_view;


d)


DISPLAY dept_view;


View AnswerAnswer: b
Explanation: Views behave like virtual tables. To see their data, we use SELECT queries against the view.
